PURPOSE:

    The purpose of this door, is to allow MAX's BBS's users, the use of a BBS
    lister. This lister allows a BBS entry to be input by SYSOP's/CO-SYSOP's
    and under certain circumstances normal USER's. This BBS list maybe listed
    in short/long form, downloaded, added to, edited and deleted, 
    (depending on the access level of the user). This door has fairly 
    comprehensive error checking/reporting, and has been fairly well tested
    on a A3000/040 WB2.04, 26 Mbyte machine, running MAX's BBS 1.52. It's
    fairly well explanatory, except for the installation, which *MUST* be
    adhered too. 

*NOTE* TWITTING now works perfectly from anywhere!!!



INSTALLATION:

    To install this fine program, make a directory in your "DOORS:" directory 
    called "BBS_Lister", then just copy to your new directory, the following 
    files.


*NOTE* If you already have a BBS_Lister.config that you want to use, then put
    the old one in this directory, and do not use this new one!


    1: BBS_Lister
    2: BBS_Lister.config
    3: BBS_Lister.data

    Your doors directory should now look thus,

    DOORS:BBS_Lister/BBS_Lister
    DOORS:BBS_Lister/BBS_Lister.config
    DOORS:BBS_Lister/BBS_Lister.data


*NOTE* The above have to be followed to the letter, ie the directory name
    must be "DOORS:BBS_Lister" and the three files must be within the 
    "DOORS:BBS_Lister" directory for the door to have any hope of working.


*NOTE* When a user downloads the BBS list, a text BBS list is created in RAM:, 
    If you haven't got RAM: ????, no one will be able to download, also if the
    number of BBS's gets really big, this file will get really big and eat up
    all your ram:. ie with 1000 BBS's the file will be exactly 328,000 bytes
    long, if anyone has problems, I can change this, but only if you're
    desperate ;-). The door does delete this file when the user, has finished
    downloading, so it is a temporary amount of ram that is needed.


    Then go about configuring your menus to allow this door to be used, ie edit
    ????menu.text files, also under configure menus, set a key and function 34,
    (door function), then set the correct path in FILENAME/PATHS etc.


    eg. KEY:  FUNCTION:  EXTRA:  LO:   HI:      FILENAME/NAME/DEST/PATH
         B       34        0      0   10000     DOORS:BBS_Lister/BBS_Lister



CONFIGURATION:

*NOTE* The BBS_Lister.data file is not editable, any editing by a standard
    editor, will render it most likely useless. A hex editor can be used, but
    there shouldn't be any need, you can edit from the door anyhow.

    The door reads a config file, that for this version contains 4 or 6 parameters.
    This file is editable by any standard editor, just make sure each parameter
    is on a seperate line, without any other characters whatsoever. 

    eg.

    1: 10000        ;range 0-32000
    2: 5000         ;range 0-32000
    3: 10           ;range 0-32000
    4: 0            ;range 0-1
    5: dh0:d/lha a  ;Optional string specifying path/file/options for compression.
    6: lha          ;Optional extension depending on option 5, ie if
                     option 5 exists, then this MUST as well.   

*NOTE* Numbers and comments only for the sake of clarity, file *HAS* to contain
       either 4 or 6 parameters, in the correct order!
           

    1: SYSOP Access level, this number sets who can use all functions within the
       door. Normally you should only allow the SYSOP of the BBS to have access
       to all the functions, so set this value to the same as the SYSOP access
       level within the BBS, nominally 10000. Anyone who has this level when they
       logon will be able to list/add/edit/delete/download any part off the BBS
       list, so choose this level wisely, you have been warned!

       
    2: CO-SYSOP Access Level, this number sets who can access a subset of all the
       functions available. Normally other user's who have their own boards, ie
       SYSOP'S in their own right, they have the ability to list/add/download any
       part off the BBS, edit certain parts, depending on parameter 4, (see
       below). Nominally set to 5000.

       
    3: MEMBER Access Level, this number sets who can access minimal functions
       within the door. Normally to allow all members access, anyone having an
       access level less than this, will not have any access to any of the 
       functions within this door, ie guests who usually have an access of 5.
       Nominally set to a value of 10.

       
    4: This number is a the DANGER switch, it decides the overall access for
       CO-SYSOP's, and MEMBERS. The SYSOP's control off this door is unaffected
       by this switch. When this switch is set to "0", Members can only 
       list/download from the BBS list, CO-SYSOP's can add 1 BBS list, in their 
       name, ie their own BBS, CO-SYSOP's can also only edit their own BBS entry.
       If this switch is set to a "1", Members can list/download and add BBS 
       entries, CO-SYSOP's can also list/download and add, but they can still 
       only edit their own BBS entry. No matter what the state of this switch,
       only the SYSOP can delete entries and edit any entry.

        
    5: This string is an optional parameter, that specifies, whether you
       want the downloaded text file to be compressed or not. If the
       string is absent, then no compression is performed and the BBS
       Lister operates in exactly the same fashion as V0.02. If a string
       is put here, then the door will execute that string. So this string
       needs to contain the full path, and options that the compression
       program you are using, normally requires! Amother stipulation, is
       that the compression program can accept a filename and generate its
       own output file with the correct extension. 

    ie. lha a ram:BBS_list ;will produce an archive called ram:BBS_list.lha

       This parameter may be upto 80 chars long, look at the examples
       below, if the above doesn't make sense.


    6: This parameter is soley dependent upon parameter 5:, in that if
       parameter 5: exists, then this one also has to exist. If parameter
       5: does not exist, then this one may not exist also. This parameter
       is very simply the extension applied to the ASCII text file, this
       extension allows the door to delete the files from ram: once the
       compression/download is finished. 



EXAMPLES:	

    eg1. (default values, use in normal situation).
       
    10000       ;User with level >=10000, has full control off door.
    5000        ;User with level >=5000, can list/dload all, add/edit one entry.
    10          ;User with level >=10, can list/dload all.
    0           ;Danger switch off, Members/CO-SYSOP's have limited control.
                ;No compression string, so file downloaded as straight ASCII          
                ;No parameter above, so this is empty as well.

    eg2.

    12000       ;User with level >=12000, has full control off door.
    1000        ;User with level >=1000, can list/dload/add all, edit one entry.
    5           ;User with level >=10, can list/dload/add all.
    1           ;Danger switch on, Members/CO-SYSOP's have more control.
    dh0:d/lha a ;Uses "lha" in path "dh0:d" with option "a", too archive.
    lha         ;"lha" extension of archive file produced by parameter 5:         
          


COMPILING:

    The following should be present in the archive!

       BBS_Lister        - 27056 bytes   ;Ari's Amiga Executable V 0.02
       BBS_Lister.doc    - 9209  bytes   ;I'm typing it, you're reading it!!!
       BBS_Lister.config - 32    bytes   ;Config file, Der!!!
       BBS_Lister.data   - 1968  bytes   ;Binary BBS list data file



